---
import QuestionLayout from '@layouts/QuestionLayout.astro';

const relatedLinks = [];
const _body = "<p>Usual first path: author in plain English with the Claude skill <code>traverse-capability-author</code> — interview → registry check → contract → WASM → human-reviewed PR. You do not need to write Rust for that path. Under the hood the artifact is still Rust→WASM.</p>\n\n      <h2>Skill-first (usual)</h2>\n      <ul>\n        <li>Install <code>traverse-capability-author</code> from <a href=\"https://github.com/traverse-framework/claude-skills\">traverse-framework/claude-skills</a></li>\n        <li>Describe the business rule; the skill checks the registry, drafts the contract, and produces executable WASM</li>\n        <li>Open the human-reviewed registry PR the skill prepares</li>\n        <li>Consume with published JS/TS (<code>traverse-embedder-web</code>), Rust (<code>traverse-embedder</code>), agents via <code>traverse-mcp</code>, or Python by shelling out to <code>traverse-cli capability-package execute</code></li>\n      </ul>\n\n      <h2>Manual CLI path (Rust)</h2>\n      <p>If you prefer to write the guest yourself: with Rust and the <code>wasm32-wasip1</code> target installed, the CLI quickstart is a short loop — <code>traverse-cli capability new</code>, fill schemas and guest logic, compile, <code>capability-package inspect</code>, then <code>capability-package execute</code>.</p>\n\n      <h2>The learning curve after that</h2>\n      <p>Getting comfortable with contracts takes a bit longer: governing specs, JSON Schema gates, and how bundles group capabilities. See <a href=\"/questions/do-i-need-rust.html\">do I need Rust</a> for the honest split between skill-first, manual authoring, and consume-only paths.</p>\n\n      <h2>Production readiness</h2>\n      <p>Getting a capability into production on the local target is straightforward. Integrating with AI agents via MCP, deploying to edge or cloud targets, and setting up a shared registry for a team takes more planning. The <a href=\"/docs/quickstart.html\">quickstart</a> and <a href=\"/questions/how-do-i-author-a-capability-in-plain-english.html\">plain-English authoring</a> pages are the right doors.</p>";
const _jsonLd = "{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"FAQPage\",\"mainEntity\":[{\"@type\":\"Question\",\"name\":\"How long does it take to get started with Traverse?\",\"acceptedAnswer\":{\"@type\":\"Answer\",\"text\":\"Usual path: author in plain English with traverse-capability-author (no Rust required for that path; under the hood still Rust→WASM), then consume via published embedders or CLI. Manual Rust CLI scaffolding remains available. Production use takes longer — contracts, bundles, placement, and registry fit.\"}}]}";
---
<QuestionLayout
  title={"How long does it take to get started with Traverse?"}
  description={"Usual start: plain-English authoring via traverse-capability-author. Manual Rust CLI path still available. Honest split of skill-first vs consume vs manual."}
  canonical={"https://traverse-framework.com/questions/how-long-to-get-started.html"}
  category={"Getting Started"}
  relatedLinks={relatedLinks}
  jsonLd={_jsonLd}
>
  <Fragment set:html={_body} />
</QuestionLayout>
